  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 231 AM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nAt last, we have arrived at the last day of the Extreme Heat\nWarning! Heat indices 105 to 110 are once again expected, as\ntemperatures climb to near 100 throughout the Quad State. No\nchanges were made to the Excessive Heat Warning this morning. \n\nWe briefly considered issuing a Heat Advisory or Extreme Heat \nWarning for Sunday over the western 4 counties in southeast \nMissouri, but decided to let the day shift take one more look at\nit. The NBM's triple digit heat indices have trended downward \nand farther west in the last 24 hours, and with an increased \nchance of convection for today and a healthy dry northeast \nfetch expected Sunday, it is no forgone conclusion that any of \nthe area will reach 100 Sunday. \n\nWe already had a few showers and even a lightning strike or two\ndevelop just northeast of the area in the last hour or so. Some\nof the 00Z and later guidance develops showers along the I-64 \ncorridor later this morning mostly after daybreak. Not sure \nexactly why that is happening, but the early morning development\nlends a bit of confidence for this later development. We may \nneed to add some small PoPs in the far north this morning.\n\nThe main concern today will be scattered showers and \nthunderstorms. There are no obvious convergent boundaries to \nfocus development, but there are a couple of possibilities \noffered up by the latest guidance. One would have scattered \nstorms developing over or just north of the Tri State after 19Z,\nand then moving south or even southwest through much of the \narea into the evening. A second possibility would be for \ndevelopment from the Perryville and Pinckneyville areas \nsoutheast into the Pennyrile also after 19Z. These storms would \nalso move southeast into evening. A combination of 00Z ARW and \n3km NAM was used to combine these two possibilities by showing  \ndevelopment after 19Z that progresses southwest through the \nentire area through the late afternoon and evening. \n\nCAPE of 2000-3000J/kg and DCAPE well over 1000J/kg will lead to\na strong potential for gusty convective winds throughout the \narea. However, 0-6km shear of 30-40kts over the Evansville Tri \nState and Pennyrile could support a few severe storms with \ndamaging winds there. Otherwise, lightning and locally heavy \nrainfall will be possible throughout the area. We could see \nquite a few outflow boundaries bouncing around the area, \nsustaining development into the evening. The convection should \nbe done by 06Z and the rest of the weekend will be dry.\n\nThe forecast has trended a bit cooler Sunday and Monday. A few\nlocations may not reach 90 degrees either day. However, the\nupper-level ridge will build right back over the Quad State by\nTuesday morning and south winds will develop at the surface\nlater Tuesday. That combination will persist through Wednesday\nwhen much of the region will see a return to triple digit heat\nindices. Right now it looks like more in the 100-105 range,\nwhich would not require a Heat Advisory.\n\nThe upper ridge will eventually break down again Wednesday night\ninto Thursday, and that will bring another decent chance of\nconvection and another cold front through the area by Friday.\nConfidence in the details for the end of the work week is low at\nthis time. However, moderate instability is reasonable to assume\nfor Thursday, and some increased mid and upper-level flow could\nsupport a few stronger storms.",
